      </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-line3">
      <title>Projection onto a line in <m>\R^3</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>x = \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
3 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad u = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and let <m>L</m> be the line spanned by <m>u</m>.  Compute <m>x_L</m> and <m>x_L^\perp</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
<mrow>x_L = \frac{x\cdot u}{u\cdot u}\,u = \frac{2+3-1}{1+1+1}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \frac{4}{3}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            x_{L^\perp} = x - x_L = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
5 \\
-7
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Orthogonal projection onto the line <m>L</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/projection-if.html?u1=-1,1,1&amp;vec=-2,3,-1&amp;labels=u&amp;range=3.5&amp;closed"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      When <m>A</m> is a matrix with more than one column, computing the orthogonal projection of <m>x</m> onto <m>W = \Col(A)</m> means solving the matrix equation <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m>.  In other words, we can compute the closest vector by <em>solving a system of linear equations</em>.  To be explicit, we state the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula"/> as a recipe:
    </p>

    <algorithm>
      <title>Compute an orthogonal decomposition</title>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>computation of</h><h>row reduction</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>W</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^m</m>.  Here is a method to compute the orthogonal decomposition of a vector <m>x</m> with respect to <m>W</m>:
        <ol start="0">
          <li><p>
            Rewrite <m>W</m> as the column space of a matrix <m>A</m>.  In other words, find a a spanning set for <m>W</m>, and let <m>A</m> be the matrix with those columns.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Compute the matrix <m>A^TA</m> and the vector <m>A^Tx</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Form the augmented matrix for the matrix equation <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m> in the unknown vector <m>c</m>, and row reduce.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            This equation is always consistent; choose one solution <m>c</m>.  Then
            <md>
<mrow>x_W = Ac \qquad x_{W^\perp} = x - x_W</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </algorithm>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-xy-plane">
      <title>Projection onto the <m>xy</m>-plane</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula"/> to compute the orthogonal decomposition of a vector with respect to the <m>xy</m>-plane in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A basis for the <m>xy</m>-plane is given by the two standard coordinate vectors
          <md>
<mrow>e_1 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} \qquad e_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Let <m>A</m> be the matrix with columns <m>e_1,e_2</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Then
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} = I_2 \qquad
            A^T\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          It follows that the unique solution <m>c</m> of <m>A^TAc = I_2c = A^Tx</m> is given by the first two coordinates of <m>x</m>, so
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = A\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            x_{W^\perp} = x - x_W = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
0 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We have recovered <xref ref="projections-eg-xy-plane"/>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-plane">
      <title>Projection onto a plane in <m>\R^3</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>W = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
            \qquad
            x = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Compute <m>x_W</m> and the distance from <m>x</m> to <m>W</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have to solve the matrix equation <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We have
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \qquad A^Tx = \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
2 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 3
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp -7/3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 8/3
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            c = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
-7 \\
8
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          It follows that
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = Ac = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
8 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            x_{W^\perp} = x - x_W = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The distance from <m>x</m> to <m>W</m> is
          <md>
<mrow>\|x_{W^\perp}\| = \frac 1{3}\sqrt{4+4+4} \approx 1.155</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Orthogonal projection onto the plane <m>W</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/projection-if.html?u1=1,0,-1&amp;u2=-1,-2,-1&amp;vec=1,2,3&amp;labels=v1,v2&amp;range=3.5&amp;closed&amp;mode=decomp"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-plane-2">
      <title>Projection onto another plane in <m>\R^3</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>W = \left\{\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}\bigm|x_1 - 2x_2 = x_3\right\}
            \sptxt{and}
            x = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Compute <m>x_W</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>Method 1:</em> First we need to find a spanning set for <m>W</m>.  We notice that <m>W</m> is the solution set of the homogeneous equation <m>x_1 - 2x_2 - x_3 = 0</m>, so <m>W = \Nul\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</m>.  We know how to compute a basis for a null space: we row reduce and find the parametric vector form.  The matrix <m>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</m> is already in reduced row echelon form.  The parametric form is <m>x_1 = 2x_2 + x_3</m>, so the parametric vector form is
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = x_2\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_3\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and hence a basis for <m>V</m> is given by
          <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
          We let <m>A</m> be the matrix whose columns are our basis vectors:
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence <m>\Col(A) = \Nul\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix} = W</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now we can continue with step 1 of the recipe.  We compute
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
5 \amp 2 \\
2 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \qquad A^Tx = \begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We write the linear system <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m> as an augmented matrix and row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
5 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
2 \amp 2 \amp 2
\end{array}\right) \rref \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1/3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 2/3
\end{array}\right)</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence we can take <m>c = {1/3\choose 2/3}</m>, so
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = Ac = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1/3 \\
2/3
\end{pmatrix} = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
1 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Orthogonal projection onto the plane <m>W</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/projection-if.html?u1=1,1,-1&amp;u2=1,0,1&amp;vec=1,1,1&amp;labels=v1,v2&amp;range=3.5&amp;closed&amp;mode=decomp"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

        <p>
          <em>Method 2:</em> In this case, it is easier to compute <m>x_{W^\perp}</m>.  Indeed, since <m>W = \Nul\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -2 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix},</m> the orthogonal complement is the line
          <md>
<mrow>V = W^\perp = \Col\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Using the formula for <xref ref="projections-onto-line">projection onto a line</xref> gives
          <md>
<mrow>x_{W^\perp} = x_V = \frac{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix} = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Hence we have
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = x - x_{W^\perp} = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} - \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}.
            = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
1 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          as above.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Projection onto a <m>3</m>-space in <m>\R^4</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
<mrow>W = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
            \qquad
            x = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Compute the orthogonal decomposition of <m>x</m> with respect to <m>W</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have to solve the matrix equation <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp -1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We compute
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            A^Tx = \begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
-3 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}
2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 4 \amp 0
\end{array}\right)
            \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3/2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3/2
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            c = \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
-6 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          It follows that
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = Ac = \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
-3 \\
6 \\
3
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            x_{W^\perp} = \frac 12\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
5 \\
0 \\
5
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      In the context of the above recipe, if we start with a <em>basis</em> of <m>W</m>, then it turns out that the square matrix <m>A^TA</m> is automatically invertible!  (It is always the case that <m>A^TA</m> is square and the equation <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m> is consistent, but <m>A^TA</m> need not be invertible in general.)
    </p>

    <corollary xml:id="projections-ATA-formula2">
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>computation of</h><h>complicated matrix formula</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Basis</h><h>and orthogonal projection</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with linearly independent columns and let <m>W = \Col(A)</m>.  Then the <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A^TA</m> is invertible, and for all vectors <m>x</m> in <m>\R^m</m>, we have
          <md>
<mrow>x_W = A(A^TA)\inv A^Tx</mrow>
</md>.
</p>
      </statement>
      <proof visible="true">
        <p>
          We will show that <m>\Nul(A^TA)=\{0\}</m>, which implies invertibility by the <xref ref="imt-2"/>.  Suppose that <m>A^TAc = 0</m>.  Then <m>A^TAc = A^T0</m>, so <m>0_W = Ac</m> by the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula"/>.  But <m>0_W = 0</m> (the orthogonal decomposition of the zero vector is just <m>0 = 0 + 0)</m>, so <m>Ac = 0</m>, and therefore <m>c</m> is in <m>\Nul(A)</m>.  Since the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, we have <m>c=0</m>, so <m>\Nul(A^TA)=0</m>, as desired.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>x</m> be a vector in <m>\R^n</m> and let <m>c</m> be a solution of <m>A^TAc = A^Tx</m>.  Then <m>c = (A^TA)\inv A^Tx</m>, so <m>x_W =Ac = A(A^TA)\inv A^Tx</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>

    <p>
          The corollary applies in particular to the case where we have a subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\R^m</m>, and a basis <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m> for <m>W</m>.  To apply the corollary, we take <m>A</m> to be the <m>m\times n</m> matrix with columns <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n</m>.
        </p>


    <example xml:id="projections-onto-plane2">
      <title>Computing a projection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Continuing with the above <xref ref="projections-onto-plane"/>, let
          <md>
<mrow>W = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}
            \qquad
            x = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          Compute <m>x_W</m> using the formula <m>x_W = A(A^TA)\inv A^Tx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Clearly the spanning vectors are noncollinear, so according to the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula2"/>, we have <m>x_W = A(A^TA)\inv A^Tx</m>, where
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We compute
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix} \implies (A^TA)\inv = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          so
          <md>
<mrow>\spalignsysdelims()\spalignsystabspace=0pt
x_W \amp= A(A^TA)\inv A^Tx
            = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix}
            = \frac 13\left\{\begin{alignedat}{3}
2x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp x_2 \amp {}-{} \amp x_3 \amp {} \\
x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp 2x_2 \amp {}+{} \amp x_3 \amp {} \\
-x_1 \amp {}+{} \amp x_2 \amp {}+{} \amp 2x_3 \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.</mrow>
</md>.
So, for example, if <m>x=(1,0,0)</m>, this formula tells us that <m>x_W = (2,1,-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-projection">
    <title>Orthogonal Projection</title>
    <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>as a transformation</h></idx>

    <p>
      In this subsection, we change perspective and think of the orthogonal projection <m>x_W</m> as a <em>function</em> of <m>x</m>.  This function turns out to be a linear transformation with many nice properties, and is a good example of a linear transformation which is not originally defined as a matrix transformation.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="projections-properties-of">
      <title>Properties of Orthogonal Projections</title>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>properties of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>linearity of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>composed with itself</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>range of</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>W</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, and define <m>T\colon\R^n\to\R^n</m> by <m>T(x) = x_W</m>.  Then:
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>T</m> is a linear transformation.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>T(x)=x</m> if and only if <m>x</m> is in <m>W</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>T(x)=0</m> if and only if <m>x</m> is in <m>W^\perp</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>T\circ T = T</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The range of <m>T</m> is <m>W</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>
              We have to verify the <xref ref="linear-trans-defn">defining properties of linearity</xref>.  Let <m>x,y</m> be vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, and let <m>x = x_W + x_{W^\perp}</m> and <m>y = y_W + y_{W^\perp}</m> be their orthogonal decompositions.  Since <m>W</m> and <m>W^\perp</m> are subspaces, the sums <m>x_W+y_W</m> and <m>x_{W^\perp}+y_{W^\perp}</m> are in <m>W</m> and <m>W^\perp</m>, respectively.  Therefore, the orthogonal decomposition of <m>x+y</m> is <m>(x_W+y_W)+(x_{W^\perp}+y_{W^\perp})</m>, so
              <md>
<mrow>T(x+y) = (x+y)_W = x_W+y_W = T(x) + T(y)</mrow>
</md>.
              Now let <m>c</m> be a scalar.  Then <m>cx_W</m> is in <m>W</m> and <m>cx_{W^\perp}</m> is in <m>W^\perp</m>, so the orthogonal decomposition of <m>cx</m> is <m>cx_W + cx_{W^\perp}</m>, and therefore,
              <md>
<mrow>T(cx) = (cx)_W = cx_W = cT(x)</mrow>
</md>.
              Since <m>T</m> satisfies the two <xref ref="linear-trans-defn">defining properties</xref>, it is a linear transformation.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              See <xref ref="projections-eg-inW"/>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              See <xref ref="projections-eg-inWperp"/>.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              For any <m>x</m> in <m>\R^n</m> the vector <m>T(x)</m> is in <m>W</m>, so <m>T\circ T(x) = T(T(x)) = T(x)</m> by 2.
            </p></li>
            <li><p>
              Any vector <m>x</m> in <m>W</m> is in the range of <m>T</m>, because <m>T(x) = x</m> for such vectors.  On the other hand, for any vector <m>x</m> in <m>\R^n</m> the output <m>T(x) = x_W</m> is in <m>W</m>, so <m>W</m> is the range of <m>T</m>.
            </p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Linear transformation</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>orthogonal projection</h></idx>
      We compute the standard matrix of the orthogonal projection in the same way as for <xref ref="matrix-of-transformation" text="title">any other transformation</xref>: by evaluating on the standard coordinate vectors.  In this case, this means projecting the standard coordinate vectors onto the subspace.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Matrix of a projection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>L</m> be the line in <m>\R^2</m> spanned by the vector <m>u = {3\choose 2}</m>, and define <m>T\colon\R^2\to\R^2</m> by <m>T(x)=x_L</m>.  Compute the standard matrix <m>B</m> for <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The columns of <m>B</m> are <m>T(e_1) = (e_1)_L</m> and <m>T(e_2) = (e_2)_L</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>\left.
(e_1)_L \amp= \frac{u\cdot e_1}{u\cdot u}\,u
            = \frac{3}{13}\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>(e_2)_L \amp= \frac{u\cdot e_2}{u\cdot u}\,u
            = \frac{2}{13}\begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>\right\} \quad\implies\quad
            B = \frac 1{13}\begin{pmatrix}
9 \amp 6 \\
6 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix}.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Matrix of a projection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>L</m> be the line in <m>\R^2</m> spanned by the vector
          <md>
<mrow>u = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          and define <m>T\colon\R^3\to\R^3</m> by <m>T(x)=x_L</m>.  Compute the standard matrix <m>B</m> for <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The columns of <m>B</m> are <m>T(e_1) = (e_1)_L</m>, <m>T(e_2) = (e_2)_L</m>, and <m>T(e_3) = (e_3)_L</m>.  We have
          <md>
<mrow>\left.
(e_1)_L \amp= \frac{u\cdot e_1}{u\cdot u}\,u
            = \frac{-1}{3}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>(e_2)_L \amp= \frac{u\cdot e_2}{u\cdot u}\,u
            = \frac{1}{3}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>(e_3)_L \amp= \frac{u\cdot e_3}{u\cdot u}\,u
            = \frac{1}{3}\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>\right\} \quad\implies\quad
            B = \frac 1{3}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-plane3">
      <title>Matrix of a projection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Continuing with <xref ref="projections-onto-plane"/>, let
          <md>
<mrow>W = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>,
          and define <m>T\colon\R^3\to\R^3</m> by <m>T(x)=x_W</m>.  Compute the standard matrix <m>B</m> for <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The columns of <m>B</m> are <m>T(e_1) = (e_1)_W</m>, <m>T(e_2) = (e_2)_W</m>, and <m>T(e_3) = (e_3)_W</m>.  Let
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          To compute each <m>(e_i)_W</m>, we solve the matrix equation <m>A^TAc = A^Te_i</m> for <m>c</m>, then use the equality <m>(e_i)_W = Ac</m>.  First we note that
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix};
            \qquad
            A^Te_i = \text{the $i$th column of } A^T = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          For <m>e_1</m>, we form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 1
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1/3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1/3
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            (e_1)_W = A\begin{pmatrix}
1/3 \\
1/3
\end{pmatrix} = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We do the same for <m>e_2</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 1
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1/3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 2/3
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            (e_1)_W = A\begin{pmatrix}
-1/3 \\
2/3
\end{pmatrix} = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
2 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
          and for <m>e_3</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 0
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp -2/3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1/3
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            (e_1)_W = A\begin{pmatrix}
-2/3 \\
1/3
\end{pmatrix} = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          It follows that
          <md>
<mrow>B = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="projections-onto-plane3"/>, we could have used the fact that
      <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>
      forms a <em>basis</em> for <m>W</m>, so that
      <md>
<mrow>T(x) = x_W = \bigl[A(A^TA)\inv A^T\bigr]x
        \sptxt{for}
        A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
      by the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula2"/>.  In this case, we have already expressed <m>T</m> as a matrix transformation with matrix <m>A(A^TA)\inv A^T</m>.  See <xref ref="projections-onto-plane2"/>.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>complicated matrix formula</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>W</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m> with basis <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m</m>, and let <m>A</m> be the matrix with columns <m>v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m</m>.  Then the standard matrix for <m>T(x) = x_W</m> is
        <md>
<mrow>A(A^TA)\inv A^T</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      We can translate the above <xref ref="projections-properties-of">properties of orthogonal projections</xref> into properties of the associated standard matrix.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="projections-matrices-properties-of">
      <title>Properties of Projection Matrices</title>
      <idx><h>Projection matrix</h><see>Orthogonal projection, standard matrix of</see></idx>
      <idx><h>Matrix</h><h>projection</h><see>Orthogonal projection, standard matrix of</see></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>properties of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>column space of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Column space</h><h>of an orthogonal projection</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>null space of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Null space</h><h>of an orthogonal projection</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>square of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>diagonalizability of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>eigenvalues of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>eigenvectors of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Orthogonal projection</h><h>standard matrix of</h><h>noninvertibility of</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvalue</h><h>of a projection matrix</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenvector</h><h>of a projection matrix</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Eigenspace</h><h>of a projection matrix</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Diagonalizability</h><h>of a projection matrix</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>W</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, define <m>T\colon\R^n\to\R^n</m> by <m>T(x) = x_W</m>, and let <m>B</m> be the standard matrix for <m>T</m>.  Then:
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>\Col(B) = W.</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\Nul(B) = W^\perp.</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>B^2 = B.</m></p></li>
	    <li><p>If <m>W \neq \{0\}</m>, then 1 is an eigenvalue of <m>B</m> and the 1-eigenspace for <m>B</m> is <m>W</m>.</p></li>
	    <li><p>If <m>W \neq \R^n</m>, then 0 is an eigenvalue of <m>B</m> and the 0-eigenspace for <m>B</m> is <m>W^\perp</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>B</m> is similar to the diagonal matrix with <m>m</m> ones and <m>n-m</m> zeros on the diagonal, where <m>m = \dim(W).</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The first four assertions are translations of <xref ref="projections-properties-of">properties 5, 3, 4, and 2</xref>, respectively, using <xref ref="matrix-trans-dictionary"/> and <xref ref="matrix-mult-comp-is-prod"/>.  The fifth assertion is equivalent to the second, by <xref ref="evecs-eval0"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the final assertion, we showed in the proof of <xref ref="projections-decomp-exists"/> that there is a basis of <m>\R^n</m> of the form <m>\{v_1,\ldots,v_m,v_{m+1},\ldots,v_n\}</m>, where <m>\{v_1,\ldots,v_m\}</m> is a basis for <m>W</m> and <m>\{v_{m+1},\ldots,v_n\}</m> is a basis for <m>W^\perp</m>.  Each <m>v_i</m> is an eigenvector of <m>B</m>: indeed, for <m>i\leq m</m> we have
          <md>
<mrow>Bv_i = T(v_i) = v_i = 1\cdot v_i</mrow>
</md>
          because <m>v_i</m> is in <m>W</m>, and for <m>i > m</m> we have
          <md>
<mrow>Bv_i = T(v_i) = 0 = 0\cdot v_i</mrow>
</md>
          because <m>v_i</m> is in <m>W^\perp</m>.  Therefore, we have found a basis of eigenvectors, with associated eigenvalues <m>1,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0</m> (<m>m</m> ones and <m>n-m</m> zeros).  Now we use the <xref ref="diagonalization-thm"/>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

<p>We emphasize that the <xref ref="projections-matrices-properties-of">properties of projection matrices</xref> would be very hard to prove in terms of matrices.  By translating all of the statements into statements about linear transformations, they become much more transparent.  For example, consider the projection matrix we found in <xref ref="projections-onto-plane3"/>.  Just by looking at the matrix it is not at all obvious that when you square the matrix you get the same matrix back.</p>

    <example xml:id="projections-onto-plane4">
      <p>
        Continuing with the above <xref ref="projections-onto-plane3"/>, we showed that
        <md>
<mrow>B = \frac 13\begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        is the standard matrix of the orthogonal projection onto
        <md>
<mrow>W = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
        One can verify by hand that <m>B^2=B</m> (try it!).  We compute <m>W^\perp</m> as the null space of
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix} \rref \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        The free variable is <m>x_3</m>, and the parametric form is <m>x_1 = x_3,\,x_2 = -x_3</m>, so that
        <md>
<mrow>W^\perp = \Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
        It follows that <m>B</m> has eigenvectors
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\qquad \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\qquad \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>
        with eigenvalues <m>1,1,0</m>, respectively, so that
        <md>
<mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <remark>
      <p>
        As we saw in <xref ref="projections-onto-plane4"/>, if you are willing to compute bases for <m>W</m> and <m>W^\perp</m>, then this provides a third way of finding the standard matrix <m>B</m> for projection onto <m>W</m>: indeed, if <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m\}</m> is a basis for <m>W</m> and <m>\{v_{m+1},v_{m+2},\ldots,v_n\}</m> is a basis for <m>W^\perp</m>, then
        <md>
<mrow>B = \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_1 \amp \cdots \amp v_n \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
\vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
0 \amp \cdots \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \\
\vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
| \amp | \amp | \\
v_1 \amp v_1 \amp \cdots \amp v_n \\
| \amp | \amp |
\end{pmatrix}\inv</mrow>
</md>,
        where the middle matrix in the product is the diagonal matrix with <m>m</m> ones and <m>n-m</m> zeros on the diagonal.  However, since you already have a basis for <m>W</m>, it is faster to multiply out the expression <m>A(A^TA)\inv A^T</m> as in the <xref ref="projections-ATA-formula2"/>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>Reflections</title>
      <idx><h>Reflection</h><h>in general</h></idx>
      <p>
        Let <m>W</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, and let <m>x</m> be a vector in <m>\R^n</m>.  The <em>reflection</em> of <m>x</m> over <m>W</m> is defined to be the vector
        <md>
<mrow>\refl_W(x) = x - 2x_{W^\perp}</mrow>
</md>.
        In other words, to find <m>\refl_W(x)</m> one starts at <m>x</m>, then moves to <m>x-x_{W^\perp} = x_W</m>, then continues in the same direction one more time, to end on the opposite side of <m>W</m>.
        </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes]
  \draw[seq-violet] (-3,-2) -- node[below right, very near start] {$W$} (3,2);
  \coordinate (x) at (-3,2);
  \coordinate (o) at (0,0);
  \draw[vector,seq-red] (o) -- node[auto,swap] {$x$} (x);
  \point[seq-blue, "$x_W$" {seq-blue, below, yshift=-2mm}] (p) at (${-2.5/(1.5*1.5+1)}*(1.5,1)$);
  \coordinate (p2) at ($-1*(x)+2*(p)$);
  \draw[vector, seq-green] (x) -- node[below left] {$-x_{W^\perp}$} (p);
  \draw[vector, seq-green] (p) -- node[below left] {$-x_{W^\perp}$} ($-1*(x)+2*(p)$);
  \draw[vector, seq-green] (p) -- node[below left] {$-x_{W^\perp}$} (p2);
  \pic[draw] {right angle=(o)--(p)--(p2)};
  \draw[vector, seq-orange] (o) -- node[right=2pt] {$\refl_W(x)$} (p2);
  \point at (o);
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
        Since <m>x_{W^\perp} = x - x_W</m>, we also have
        <md>
<mrow>\refl_W(x) = x - 2(x - x_W) = 2x_W - x</mrow>
</md>.
        We leave it to the reader to check using the definition that:
        <ol>
          <li><p><m>\refl_W\circ\refl_W = \Id_{\R^n}.</m></p></li>
          <li><p>The <m>1</m>-eigenspace of <m>\refl_W</m> is <m>W</m>, and the <m>-1</m>-eigenspace of <m>\refl_W</m> is <m>W^\perp</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>\refl_W</m> is similar to the diagonal matrix with <m>m = \dim(W)</m> ones on the diagonal and <m>n-m</m> negative ones.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </remark>

  </subsection>

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>True or false.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>The solution to <m>A^T A x = A^T b</m> is the point in <m>\Col(A)</m> that is closest to <m>b</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>A</m> has two equal columns, then <m>A^TAx = A^Tb</m> has infinitely many solutions for every <m>b</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> are both solutions to <m>A^TAx = A^Tb</m>, then <m>v-w</m> is in the null space of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>b_L</m> and <m>b_{L^{\perp}}</m> if <m>b = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>L</m> is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>b_W</m> if <m>b = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>W</m> is the span of the vectors <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Find the distance from <m>b</m> to <m>W</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the matrix <m>A</m> for orthogonal projection onto the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  What are the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>?  What is <m>A^{101}</m>?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <md><mrow>W = \left\{\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} \textup{ in } {\bf R}^3 \ : \ x_1 -
            x_2 - x_3 = 0\right\}</mrow></md>,
        and let <m>B</m> be the matrix for orthogonal
        projection onto <m>W</m>.  Which of the following statements must be true?
        Circle all that apply.
      </p>
      <p>(i) The null space of <m>B</m> is <m>2</m>-dimensional.</p>
      <p>(ii) Suppose <m>v</m> is in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> but is not in <m>W</m>.  Then the
      closest vector to <m>v</m> in <m>W</m> is <m>Bv</m>.</p>
      <p>(iii) If <m>x</m> is a vector in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>, then <m>x</m> must be in <m>W</m> or
      <m>W^{\perp}</m>.</p>
      <p>(iv) <m>B^2 = B</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>By properties of orthogonal projections we know that (ii) and (iv)
        are true.</p>
      <p>However, (i) is false since the null
        space of <m>B</m> is <m>W^{\perp}</m> which is <m>1</m>-dimensional.  Statement
        (iii) is false: <m>x = (1, 2, 0)</m> is not in <m>W</m> (it doesn't satisfy
        the equation for <m>W</m>) and is not in <m>W^{\perp}</m> since it is not
        orthogonal to the vector <m>w = (1, 1, 0)</m> in <m>W</m>:
        <m>x \cdot w = 1(1) + 1(2) = 3 \neq 0</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Compute the matrix <m>B</m> for orthogonal projection onto the line spanned
      by <m>\begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ 3 \\ -4\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>u = \begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ 3 \\ -4\end{pmatrix}</m>, the matrix <m>B</m> is
        <md><mrow>B = \dfrac{1}{u \cdot u} uu^T = \dfrac{1}{(-5)^2+3^3+(-4)^2}
            \begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ 3 \\ -4\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}-5 \amp 3 \amp -4\end{pmatrix}
          = \dfrac{1}{50} \begin{pmatrix}25 \amp -15 \amp 20 \\ -15 \amp 9 \amp -12 \\ 20 \amp -12 \amp 16\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be the matrix for orthogonal projection onto the line <m>W</m>
      drawn below.</p>
      <image source="images/6_4/projj">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
      <p>Find
      <m>A \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>There are plenty of ways to do this problem.  One way is to draw the
        orthogonal projection of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m> onto the line and find that
        this projection is <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        Another way is to observe that the orthogonal decomposition of
          <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m> with respect to <m>W</m> is <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + {1 -2}</m>, so by
          linearity:
        <md><mrow>A \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} = A \left(\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -2\end{pmatrix}\right)
            = A \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + A \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + 0 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A third way to do this problem is to compute <m>A</m> and then
          compute <m>A \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>:
        <md><mrow>A = \frac{1}{\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} =
            \frac{1}{5} \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad A \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} =
            \dfrac{1}{5}\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, and let <m>W</m> be the line <m>y = -x</m>.
      Compute the orthogonal projection of <m>v</m> onto <m>W</m>.  Draw <m>v</m>, <m>W</m>,
      <m>v_W</m>, and <m>v_{\perp}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>W</m> be the plane in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> given by the equation
        <md><mrow>3x - y - 6z = 0</mrow></md>.
        Find the standard matrix <m>B</m> for orthogonal
        projection onto <m>W</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We could either find a basis for <m>W</m> by noting
          <m>W = \textup{Nul} \begin{pmatrix}3 \amp -1 \amp -6\end{pmatrix}</m> and row-reducing the corresponding
          augmented matrix, or we could just use the Basis Theorem by finding
          two vectors that satisfy <m>3x-y-6z=0</m>, such as:
        <md><mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow></md>.
        Setting <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, we have <m>B = A(A^TA)^{-1} A^T</m>.
          We calculate <m>(A^T A)^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{46}\begin{pmatrix}5 \amp -2 \\ -2 \amp 10\end{pmatrix}</m>
          and
        <md><mrow>B = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T = \dfrac{1}{46} \begin{pmatrix}37 \amp 3 \amp 18 \\ 3 \amp 45 \amp -6 \\ 18 \amp -6 \amp 10\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false. Justify your answer!
      Suppose that
      <m>P: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> and <m>Q: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> are
      orthogonal projections.  Then the product <m>PQ</m> must be an orthogonal
      projection.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        False.  If we take <m>P</m> to be projection onto the <m>x</m>-axis and <m>Q</m>
          to be projection onto the line <m>y=x</m>, then
        <md>
          <mrow>PQ
            \amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1/2 \amp 1/2</mrow>
          <mrow>1/2 \amp 1/2\end{pmatrix}
              = \begin{pmatrix}1/2 \amp 1/2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>(PQ)^2
            \amp  = \begin{pmatrix}1/2 \amp 1/2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1/2 \amp 1/2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}=
              \begin{pmatrix}1/4 \amp 1/4</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
        <m>PQ</m> is not the same as <m>(PQ)^2</m>, so <m>PQ</m> is not a projection.
      </p>
      <p>This means that if we take the composition of
        linear transformations that are orthogonal projections onto
        different subspaces, then the composition is not necessarily an
        orthogonal projection.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false. Justify your answer!
      Suppose that
      <m>P: {\bf R}^n \to {\bf R}^n</m> is an orthogonal projections.  Then <m>I-P</m>
      is an orthogonal projection.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        True.  If <m>P</m> is the orthogonal projection onto a subspace <m>W</m>, then
          <m>I-P</m> is the orthogonal projection onto <m>W^{\perp}</m> since <m>I-P</m>
          destroys every vector in <m>W</m> and fixes every vector in <m>W^{\perp}</m>:
          if <m>w</m> is in <m>W</m> then <m>(I-P)(w) = w - Pw = w - w = 0</m>, and if <m>y</m> is
          in <m>W^{\perp}</m> then <m>(I-P)(y) = y - Py = y - 0 = y</m>.  Alternatively,
          we could use the properties <m>P^T = P</m> and <m>P^2 = P</m> to verify that
          <m>I-P</m> is an orthogonal projection abstractly through the fact that
          <m>(I-P)^T = I^T-P^T = I-P</m> and
        <md><mrow>(I-P)^2 = (I-P)(I-P) = I^2 - IP - PI +P^2 = I-P-P+P = I-P</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
